Tobias Wolter wrote on Tue, 11 Nov 2003 11:03:42 +0100:
> On 2003-11-10, Stanislav Traykov <news@tortoises.org> wrote:
>> Do you mean that while it's useless to put on the dark protective
>> glasses during the _total_ eclipse part, one should better look
>> through 100%-UV-filtering glass? I think I have heard a similar thing
>> somewhere, but can't really see why this should be so.

> Simple glass will do. So if you're wearing spectacles anyway, you
> usually won't have a problem, at least if they cover your eyes.

Why should one need this? UVA/UVB rates drop almost to 0 during the
eclipse (as google showed easily). The crystalline lens & cornea OTOH
safely absorb these rays under normal conditions, like looking at the
snow (it reflects ~85% of UV light) for some periods of time (not
nearly as short as an eclipse lasts). Only UVB gets filtered reliably
by normal glass anyway, ~80% IIRC.

